// Package ouiregistry resolves a MAC address to the manufacturer the IEEE
// registry lists for it, using the WHOLE registry rather than a curated subset.
//
// # Inputs
//
// The embedded table (registry_gen.tsv, with canonical_gen.go) is generated by
// scripts/generate-oui-registry.mjs from three committed inputs:
//
//   - standards/oui/ieee-{mal,mam,mas}.tsv: a vendored snapshot of the IEEE
//     MA-L (24-bit), MA-M (28-bit) and MA-S (36-bit) registries, refreshed by an
//     operator with `make refresh-oui` and never hand-edited;
//   - standards/oui/vendors.yaml: the human-curated map from the registry's many
//     spellings of one manufacturer ("Cisco Systems, Inc", "Cisco SPVTG") to a
//     single canonical name, plus pins.
//
// Nothing here reaches the network: the table is compiled in.
//
// # Resolution
//
// [Lookup] picks the LONGEST covering prefix: a 36-bit MA-S block, then a
// 28-bit MA-M block, then the 24-bit MA-L block.
//
// A pin in vendors.yaml claims a prefix by name whatever its registrant (Aruba
// access points registered to Hewlett Packard Enterprise; the Hyper-V guest
// pool inside Microsoft's registrations; locally-administered conventions such
// as QEMU's 52:54:00, which the IEEE does not list at all). A pin beats the
// registrant mapping of its own prefix.
//
// Otherwise the registrant is canonicalised when vendors.yaml names it
// ([Entry.Canonical] is then true), and kept in its cleaned IEEE spelling when
// it does not.
//
// # "" means not determined
//
// Blocks the IEEE lists as "IEEE Registration Authority" (a block it has split
// into smaller assignments) or "Private" (the registrant chose not to be
// named) are not in the table, nor are the few assignments the IEEE lists under
// registrants that resolve to different vendors. For those, and for any prefix
// that is not registered, [VendorForMAC] returns "". That answer means NOT
// DETERMINED and must never be rendered as "Unknown": "the registry does not
// say" and "we did not look" are different facts.
//
// Locally-administered MACs are looked up like any other — which is how
// 52:54:00 resolves to QEMU — and a randomised phone MAC normally misses, which
// is correct. Multicast and all-zero addresses are never found.
//
// The sensor must not import this package (scripts/test-sensor-no-ouiregistry.mjs
// enforces it): vendor resolution happens on the platform, at ingestion.
package ouiregistry
